{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789229520,"nonce":"zKOrpQLbrq/plhXRAeHr/teYLAk=","message":"Fixed in d716e21.\n\nAll three engines scale by layout::array_elem_size of the HREF's tparam kind (LLVM at pointer width, so a large index does not wrap in i32). The fixture (TestRefOffset: Int, UI16, Float, I64, String refs over NativeArray data plus Int/Float refs over locals) then exposed that the interpreter's Unref/Setref treated every ref target as a NaN-boxed register slot: an Int element read as an aligned i64 (misaligned-pointer panic in ssa.rs), and a Setref wrote 8 tagged bytes over two elements. Both walkers now test whether the pointer lies in a frame's RegisterFile (this stack or a suspended thread's) and otherwise access raw memory at the kind's width; a zero word is null, and a register slot's own box (I64, null) is kept as it is.\n\nVerified on interp (SSA walker, ASH_AIR=off, ASH_AIR=v2-serialize), --mode jit, hybrid cranelift/llvm at threshold 1, AOT on aarch64 and x86-64; the review's probes (ref from a blocked thread, null through a String ref, local I64 through a ref, Dynamic array refs) agree with the compiled tiers; NUC conformance 1069/1069 on all four engines.\n\nKnown cost: ref_targets_register is a linear scan of the frames on every Unref/Setref, so a raw-memory ref dereferenced in deep recursion pays for the depth (300k derefs: 0.017s at depth 1, 0.27s at depth 900 in the review's probe). Fine until a program does that in a hot loop; the fix would be an interval set of register files maintained on push/pop.","files":null}]}